% ============================================================
% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:txn-intro}

Modern compiler optimization pipelines consist of dozens---sometimes
hundreds---of transformation passes, each of which modifies the intermediate
representation in some way.  These passes are typically executed in a fixed
order, and each pass assumes that the IR is in a consistent state when it
begins.  If a pass encounters an error (an assertion failure, an internal
compiler error, or a verification failure), the entire compilation is typically
abandoned.

\emph{Transactional compiler transformations} apply the principles of database
transactions to compiler optimization.  Each transformation is executed as a
\emph{transaction}: an atomic, consistent, isolated, and durable unit of work.
If the transformation succeeds, its effects are committed.  If it fails, its
effects are rolled back, and the IR is restored to its pre-transformation state.

This approach enables several powerful capabilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Speculative optimization:} Try an optimization and roll back if
      it does not improve the program.
\item \textbf{Concurrent optimization:} Run multiple transformations in parallel
      on different parts of the IR, using transactional conflict detection to
      ensure correctness.
\item \textbf{Incremental compilation:} Roll back and re-apply transformations
      when the source program changes.
\item \textbf{Fault tolerance:} Recover from transformation failures without
      losing the work of previous passes.
\end{itemize}

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of transactional compiler
transformations, from the basic concepts (\cref{sec:txn-concepts}) through
conflict detection (\cref{sec:txn-conflict}) and rollback (\cref{sec:txn-rollback})
to applications in concurrent and incremental compilation
(\cref{sec:txn-concurrent}, \cref{sec:txn-incremental}).

% ============================================================
% Optimizations as Transactions
% ============================================================
\section{Optimizations as Transactions}\label{sec:txn-concepts}

\subsection{The Transactional Model}\label{sec:txn-model}

\begin{definition}[Compiler Transaction]\label{def:txn}
A \emph{compiler transaction} is a transformation $T : \mathit{IR} \to \mathit{IR}$
that satisfies the ACID properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Atomicity:} Either all changes made by $T$ are applied, or none
      are.
\item \textbf{Consistency:} If the IR is valid before $T$, it is valid after $T$
      (assuming $T$ is a semantics-preserving transformation).
\item \textbf{Isolation:} Concurrent transactions do not interfere with each
      other.
\item \textbf{Durability:} Once a transaction is committed, its effects persist.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Transactional Pass Structure}\label{sec:txn-pass}

A transactional optimization pass has three phases:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Begin:} Record the current state of the IR (or the parts that
      will be modified).
\item \textbf{Execute:} Perform the transformation, recording all changes in a
      \emph{write set}.
\item \textbf{Commit or abort:} If the transformation succeeds, commit the
      changes.  If it fails, abort and restore the IR from the saved state.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Transactional Optimization Pass}\label{alg:txn-pass}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR state $S$, transformation $T$
\Ensure Updated IR state or original $S$
\State $S_{\mathit{saved}} \gets \mathit{snapshot}(S)$
\State $\mathit{writes} \gets \emptyset$
\State $\mathit{begin\_txn}()$
\State $S' \gets T(S, \mathit{writes})$
\If{$T$ succeeded}
  \State $\mathit{commit\_txn}(\mathit{writes})$
  \State \Return $S'$
\Else
  \State $\mathit{abort\_txn}(S_{\mathit{saved}})$
  \State \Return $S$
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% ============================================================
% Conflict Detection
% ============================================================
\section{Conflict Detection}\label{sec:txn-conflict}

\subsection{The Conflict Problem}\label{sec:txn-conflict-problem}

When multiple transactions run concurrently, they may conflict: two transactions
may attempt to modify the same part of the IR.  Conflict detection ensures that
such conflicts are identified and resolved.

\begin{definition}[Conflict]\label{def:conflict}
Two transactions $T_1$ and $T_2$ \emph{conflict} if they both access the same
IR element and at least one access is a write.  Formally, a conflict exists if:
\[
\mathit{writes}(T_1) \cap (\mathit{reads}(T_2) \cup \mathit{writes}(T_2)) \neq \emptyset
\]
or symmetrically for $T_2$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Granularity of Conflicts}\label{sec:txn-granularity}

The granularity at which conflicts are detected affects both correctness and
performance:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Function-level:} Conflicts are detected at the level of entire
      functions.  Two transactions conflict if they modify the same function.
      Simple but coarse-grained.
\item \textbf{Basic-block-level:} Conflicts are detected at the level of basic
      blocks.  More fine-grained but more overhead.
\item \textbf{Instruction-level:} Conflicts are detected at the level of
      individual instructions.  Most fine-grained but highest overhead.
\item \textbf{SSA-value-level:} Conflicts are detected at the level of SSA
      values (definitions).  Natural for SSA-based IRs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Optimistic Concurrency Control}\label{sec:txn-occ}

\emph{Optimistic concurrency control} (OCC) allows transactions to proceed
without locking, checking for conflicts only at commit time.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Optimistic Concurrency Control}\label{alg:occ}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Transaction $T$ with read set $R$ and write set $W$
\Ensure Committed or aborted
\State \Comment{Execution phase: execute $T$ without locking}
\State Execute $T$, recording $R$ and $W$
\State \Comment{Validation phase: check for conflicts}
\For{each $w \in W$}
  \If{$w$ was modified by another committed transaction since $T$ began}
    \State \Return \textsc{Abort}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Comment{Commit phase: apply writes}
\State Apply $W$ to the IR
\State \Return \textsc{Commit}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Rejecting a Stale Analysis Across Disjoint Writes}

Two compiler transactions can touch different output functions and still
conflict. An inliner may create a new body for a caller based on the
observed body of a callee. If another transaction edits the callee in
the meantime, checking only for competing writes to the caller would
miss the obsolete read. A compiler transaction should record the
dependencies of its analyses as well as the locations of its edits.

\textbf{Contract.} Each worker reads from one consistent immutable
snapshot and builds a private patch. Every object read or replaced
has a recorded snapshot version; version numbers never repeat.
Treat derived facts as reads of their underlying objects, and represent
queries about absent entries with a versioned container. Commit either
installs the whole patch or requests a retry. The commit lock also
guards any mutation and snapshot acquisition. The worker preallocates
the patch so installation under the lock cannot fail partway.

\begin{sudocode}[Validate the complete observed version set]
\State acquire commit lock
\To{Check observations, including objects not in the patch.}
\For{each recorded pair $(x,v)$}
  \If{$\mathrm{CurrentVersion}[x]\ne v$}
    \State release commit lock
    \State \Return retry
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\To{No other writer may intervene after validation.}
\For{each private edit $(x,b)$}
  \State install content $b$ for $x$ with a fresh version
\EndFor
\State release commit lock
\State \Return committed
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Let caller \(F\) have version \(4\) and constant
callee \(G\) have version \(7\), returning \(5\). A worker prepares
an inlined \(F\) returning \(5\). A concurrent source-update transaction
changes \(G\) to return \(9\) at version \(8\). The first worker now
returns retry because it read \((G,7)\), even though \(F\) is still at
version \(4\). A retry based on the new snapshot can inline \(9\).

When all comparisons succeed, every observed object still has the
snapshot content at the commit point. The lock makes the resulting
installation indivisible to other workers. Validation uses
\(O(|R\cup W|)\) lookups for read and write sets \(R,W\); private
construction is outside the lock. Granularity matters: a version on a
whole symbol table catches absent-name dependencies but invalidates
unrelated lookups as well.

\subsection{Conflict Resolution}\label{sec:txn-resolution}

When a conflict is detected, the transaction must be resolved:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Abort and retry:} Abort the conflicting transaction and retry it.
\item \textbf{Priority-based:} The transaction with higher priority (e.g., the
      one that has done more work) wins; the other aborts.
\item \textbf{Merge:} If the conflicting modifications are compatible (e.g.,
      they modify different parts of the same function), merge them.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Rollback and Recovery
% ============================================================
\section{Rollback and Recovery}\label{sec:txn-rollback}

\subsection{Snapshot-Based Rollback}\label{sec:txn-snapshot}

The simplest rollback mechanism is to take a full snapshot of the IR before the
transaction and restore it on abort.  This is simple but expensive for large IRs.

\subsection{Undo-Log-Based Rollback}\label{sec:txn-undolog}

A more efficient approach is to maintain an \emph{undo log}: a record of all
changes made by the transaction, in reverse order.  On abort, the undo log is
played in reverse to restore the IR.

\begin{definition}[Undo Log Entry]\label{def:undo-log}
An undo log entry is a tuple $(\mathit{loc}, \mathit{old\_val})$ recording that
the IR element at location $\mathit{loc}$ was changed from $\mathit{old\_val}$
to a new value.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Undo-Log Rollback}\label{alg:undo-log}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Undo log $L = [(l_1, v_1), \ldots, (l_n, v_n)]$
\Ensure IR restored to pre-transaction state
\For{$i = n$ down to $1$}
  \State Restore $l_i$ to value $v_i$
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Persistent Transactions}\label{sec:txn-persistent}

For incremental compilation, transactions must be \emph{persistent}: their
effects must survive across compiler invocations.  This requires writing the
committed changes to disk (or to a persistent data structure) and maintaining
the undo log for potential future rollback.

% ============================================================
% Atomicity of Transformations
% ============================================================
\section{Atomicity of Transformations}\label{sec:txn-atomicity}

\subsection{What Makes a Transformation Atomic?}\label{sec:txn-atomic-def}

A transformation is \emph{atomic} if it appears to execute instantaneously:
other transactions see either the pre-transformation state or the
post-transformation state, never an intermediate state.

Ensuring atomicity requires careful handling of the IR's data structures:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Consistent snapshots:} The snapshot must capture a consistent
      state of the IR (not a partially modified state).
\item \textbf{Atomic commit:} The commit must apply all changes atomically
      (or at least ensure that other transactions cannot observe partial
      changes).
\item \textbf{Isolation levels:} Different transactions may require different
      isolation levels (read-committed, serializable, etc.).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Nested Transactions}\label{sec:txn-nested}

Complex transformations may consist of multiple sub-transformations, each of
which should be atomic.  \emph{Nested transactions} allow a transaction to
contain sub-transactions that can be committed or aborted independently.

\begin{definition}[Nested Transaction]\label{def:nested-txn}
A \emph{nested transaction} $T_n$ within a parent transaction $T_p$ has the
following semantics:
\begin{itemize}
\item If $T_n$ commits, its changes become part of $T_p$'s write set.
\item If $T_n$ aborts, its changes are rolled back within $T_p$, but $T_p$
      continues.
\item If $T_p$ aborts, all changes (including those from committed
      sub-transactions) are rolled back.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

