\subsection{Inter-Procedural Conflicts}\label{sec:txn-ip-conflicts}

Some optimization passes are inherently inter-procedural (e.g., inlining,
inter-procedural constant propagation).  These passes cannot be trivially
parallelized by function.  Transactional conflict detection handles this by
tracking which functions are read and written by each pass:

\begin{itemize}
\item An inlining pass that inlines $f$ into $g$ reads $f$ and writes $g$.
\item If another pass is simultaneously modifying $f$ or $g$, a conflict is
      detected and one transaction must abort.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Work Stealing}\label{sec:txn-work-stealing}

For load balancing, a work-stealing scheduler can be used: each worker thread
has a queue of functions to optimize.  When a worker's queue is empty, it
\emph{steals} work from another worker's queue.  Transactions ensure that
stolen work is not duplicated.

