% Applications to Incremental Compilation
% ============================================================
\section{Applications to Incremental Compilation}\label{sec:txn-incremental}

\subsection{Incremental Compilation as Transaction Replay}\label{sec:txn-incremental}

Incremental compilation recompiles only the parts of a program that have
changed.  Transactional transformations provide a natural framework for
incremental compilation:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Each compilation pass is a transaction that transforms the IR.
\item When the source changes, the transactions for the affected functions are
      rolled back.
\item The affected functions are recompiled, and the transactions are re-applied.
\item Transactions for unaffected functions are not re-executed.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Dependency Tracking}\label{sec:txn-deps}

To determine which transactions must be rolled back when the source changes, the
compiler must track \emph{dependencies} between transactions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Source dependencies:} A transaction depends on the source code it
      read.  If the source changes, the transaction must be re-executed.
\item \textbf{IR dependencies:} A transaction depends on the IR state produced
      by previous transactions.  If a previous transaction is rolled back, all
      dependent transactions must also be rolled back.
\item \textbf{Analysis dependencies:} A transaction depends on analysis results
      (e.g., points-to information).  If the analysis is invalidated, the
      transaction must be re-executed.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Dependency Graph]\label{def:dep-graph}
The \emph{dependency graph} is a DAG where nodes are transactions and edges
represent dependencies.  When a source change invalidates a transaction $T$,
all transactions reachable from $T$ in the dependency graph must be rolled back
and re-executed.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Selective Re-Optimization}\label{sec:txn-selective}

Not all transactions need to be re-executed after a source change.  If the
change is small (e.g., modifying a constant), only transactions that depend on
the changed value need to be re-executed.  The dependency graph enables
\emph{selective re-optimization}: re-executing only the minimum set of
transactions needed to bring the IR up to date.

\begin{insight}
The transactional model unifies several seemingly different compiler
capabilities: speculative optimization, concurrent optimization, incremental
compilation, and fault tolerance.  In each case, the key idea is the same:
treat each transformation as an atomic unit of work that can be committed or
rolled back.  This abstraction enables the compiler to be more flexible, more
robust, and more efficient.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The idea of applying transactional concepts to compilers has appeared in several
contexts.  The concept of \emph{optimistic optimization}---trying an
optimization and rolling back if it fails---dates to the early 2000s in the
context of Java JIT compilation.  The \emph{Equality Saturation} framework
(\cref{ch:equality-saturation}) can be viewed as a form of transactional
optimization, where each rewrite is a transaction that is committed only if it
improves the program.  The application of transactional concepts to incremental
compilation is more recent, driven by the need for fast build times in large
codebases.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:txn-summary}

Transactional compiler transformations bring the power and flexibility of
database transactions to compiler optimization.  By treating each transformation
as an atomic, rollbackable unit of work, the compiler can speculate, parallelize,
and recover from failures in ways that are impossible with traditional
pass-based pipelines.

\subsection{Relationship to Equality Saturation}\label{sec:txn-equality}

The equality saturation framework (\cref{ch:equality-saturation}) can be viewed
as a form of transactional optimization.  Each rewrite rule application is a
transaction that adds an equivalent expression to the e-graph.  The extraction
phase selects the best expression, effectively committing the best transaction.
The key difference is that equality saturation maintains all equivalent forms
simultaneously, while transactional optimization commits or rolls back each
transformation independently.

