% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:txn-summary}

Transactional compiler transformations bring the power and flexibility of
database transactions to compiler optimization.  By treating each transformation
as an atomic, rollbackable unit of work, the compiler can speculate, parallelize,
and recover from failures in ways that are impossible with traditional
pass-based pipelines.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Overhead:} The cost of snapshotting, logging, and conflict
      detection must be kept low relative to the optimization work.
\item \textbf{Granularity:} Choosing the right granularity for transactions
      (too coarse limits parallelism; too fine increases overhead).
\item \textbf{Integration:} Retrofitting existing compiler pipelines with
      transactional support.
\item \textbf{Verification:} Proving that concurrent transactions preserve
      program semantics.
\end{itemize}
